Ikna (the web app, the Android app, their source code, sample decks, texts,
icons and other content, together "the Software") is free to use, but it is
not open source. The source code is published so people can see how it works
and trust what it does with their data.

1. What you may do

   Free of charge, you may:
   a. download, install and use the Software, on as many of your own devices
      as you like, for your own personal, non-commercial learning;
   b. use it in non-commercial teaching, for example a teacher using Ikna with
      a class, as long as nobody is charged for access to Ikna itself;
   c. read the source code;
   d. make backups of your own Ikna data, and share the cards and decks you
      made yourself.

2. What you may not do

   Without prior written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:
   a. copy, reproduce, republish or redistribute the Software or any part of
      its source code, except for the copies made automatically when you
      install and run it yourself;
   b. modify the Software, or make, publish or distribute anything based on it
      (a "derivative work"), including a renamed or rebranded version;
   c. use the Software, or any part of it, for commercial purposes. This
      includes selling it, charging for access to it, including it in a paid
      product or service, showing ads in or around it, or using it to make
      money in any other way;
   d. remove or change this license or any copyright notice;
   e. use the name "Ikna" or the Ikna icon for another product.

3. GitHub

   Because this repository is public, GitHub's Terms of Service let GitHub
   users view and fork it on GitHub. A fork on GitHub gives no rights beyond
